{
  "cumulative_conservative_charge_seconds": 22.0,
  "derivation": [
    "Let L,K be nonnegative lab kinetic energies in keV, mu uniform on [-1,1], 0<=exc<=0.100 and r>=0 a common state-dependent removal. The same normalized spectral measure is used at every nominal energy, species and transport depth.",
    "E=(L-K)/2-sqrt(L*K)*mu+exc-r = L+exc-r-(sqrt(K)-sqrt(L))^2/2-sqrt(L*K)*(1+mu) <= L+exc. Rates are zero for E<=0.",
    "For E>0, F(E,L)=sigma_Eq7(E,0.040)*sqrt(2E/L)=sqrt(2/L)*S_BH(E)/sqrt(E+0.040)*exp(-BG/sqrt(E+0.040)). It has finite positive right limit at zero. The zero-forbidden-side extension is nondecreasing. dlogF/dE=S'/S+(BG-sqrt(E+U))/(2*(E+U)^(3/2))>0: exact rational BH coefficient bounds give S,Sprime>0 on [0,16], and BG^2>16+U.",
    "At mu=-1, E>0 implies sqrt(K)<sqrt(L)+sqrt(2L+2exc); hence any proton-active state has K<Kcap=(sqrt(Lmax)+sqrt(2Lmax+2excmax))^2. For arbitrary r>=0 it also has r<Lmax+excmax.",
    "Numerator for any proton-active state is <=A=sum_i w_i F(L_i+excmax,L_i). On the reference angular half mu in [-1,0], E>=(Lref-Kcap)/2-rmax, with rmax=0 or Lmax+excmax. Keep only positive reference nodes; B=sum_j wref_j F((Lref_j-Kcap)/2-rmax,Lref_j)/2>0.",
    "If q is the measure of proton-active states, N<=q*A and D>=q*B; the remaining states add zero N and nonnegative D. Thus N<=(A/B)*D for every normalized measure. With D>0 this bounds N/D independently of n(K). If D=0, N=0 and the reported ratio is undefined, not a finite prediction. Actual common.readout(A,B) supplies the unchanged stitch, focusing and both current powers.",
    "Premises: the finite pinned transport quadrature/profile, positive weights, isotropy conditional on spectral state, common energy/depth-independent spectral measure, 0<=exc<=100eV, common nonnegative angle/beam-independent removal (possibly K-dependent recoil), equal masses/infinite spectator, and unchanged on-shell 40eV DD amplitude. This is not an exact many-body/off-shell nuclear theorem or a continuum transport enclosure. No site/density/profile/S-factor parameters are fitted."
  ],
